Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A worn fifth-floor Milwaukee walk-up. NARRATOR, a slight young man with sandy brown hair and a pizza-stained work jacket, carries groceries beside his huge older brother NICK: reddish-brown wild hair, scars on chin and left brow, heavy boots.

(SOUND: grocery bags rustle, footsteps pound up the stairwell)

NARRATOR V.O.
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

NICK shoulders six bags at once. NARRATOR struggles with two.

NARRATOR V.O.
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. Me and Ma lived here after Dad took off.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - EVENING

MA, an aging woman with soft brown hair and tired brown eyes, sits before a game show beneath a crocheted afghan. Pill bottles stand in a careful line on the kitchen counter.

(SOUND: game-show applause, television chatter)

NICK enters in his brown suede jacket, keys jangling.

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

NARRATOR
I don’t know. Why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day like that.

NARRATOR V.O.
Nick was right. Somebody had to take care of Ma. But he never let me forget whose job he thought it was.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Nick’s burnt-orange 1980 Pontiac Trans Am, yellow flames along its sides and eagle on its hood, idles at the curb. Nick enters the gym first; NARRATOR follows.

(SOUND: Trans Am engine rumbles, weights clank behind the glass)

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

A muscular lifter abandons a curl bench as Nick approaches. Nick takes the fifty-pound bar and curls it easily.

(SOUND: metal bar drops onto rubber flooring)

NARRATOR V.O.
At Moxie’s, people moved out of Nick’s way. I figured he would always stand up for me.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

NARRATOR parks his bicycle outside a modest pizza shop. SIONNA, pale and slender, with short brown hair, watery light-blue eyes, a green backpack, black V-neck shirt, jeans, and blue tennis shoes, trips on broken pavement and brushes against him.

(SOUND: bicycle chain ticks, traffic hisses over wet pavement)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That’s different.

SIONNA
My parents named me for an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

NARRATOR studies her eyes as she brushes dust from her shirt.

NARRATOR
Want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

NARRATOR V.O.
No girl had ever been interested in me. It was like I knew her already.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

A Packers game flickers above a smoky bar. Nick slaps NARRATOR on the back. NARRATOR nurses one beer, uneasy.

(SOUND: football crowd roars from television, pool balls crack)

NICK
What’s the matter? I can tell you’re hidin’ something.

NARRATOR
Ma got confused. Asked if we went to church. She couldn’t find pills right in front of her.

NICK
Why don’t you take her to the store or something? Do something before she turns into some demented old fart.

NARRATOR
I just needed to get away from her for a while.

NICK
That’s more like it. Get home.

NARRATOR exits into the cold, then stops before the illuminated public library across the street.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Old lamps glow over long wooden tables. NARRATOR approaches LIBRARIAN MARGARET, a gray-haired woman in a cardigan, at the circulation desk.

(SOUND: pages turn, radiator hisses)

NARRATOR
How do I take a book home from here?

LIBRARIAN MARGARET
Do you have a library card?

NARRATOR
No. How do I get one?

LIBRARIAN MARGARET hands him an application. Later, NARRATOR wanders through CLASSIC LITERATURE and takes down Wuthering Heights.

NARRATOR V.O.
Who needed school when everything was right here for free?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Students fill a coffee shop with notebooks and backpacks. SIONNA sits at a small rear table in a loose green hooded top. NARRATOR approaches with Wuthering Heights tucked under his arm.

(SOUND: espresso machine steams, ceramic cups clink)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. It’s about a guy and girl who grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
The love Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes. In the end, that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR listens, startled by her confidence.

SIONNA
The moors are wild and unpredictable, like the people. A love that never changes can destroy you.

NARRATOR V.O.
Something inside me was coming awake.

EXT. CITY PARK - DAY

NARRATOR and SIONNA walk beneath autumn trees. She wears a pale green coat; he carries library books. They stop by a pond.

(SOUND: wind moves dry leaves, distant children shout)

SIONNA
Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world.

NARRATOR
I feel like I want to punch people sometimes.

SIONNA
You can see the feeling. That means you can let it go.

NARRATOR
You make it sound easy.

SIONNA
It’s practice. Yoga is practice. Meditation is practice. You never have to be perfect.

NARRATOR smiles, less certain but moved.

NARRATOR
You’re really something, you know that?

SIONNA
You’re really something too.

They kiss slowly beneath the turning leaves.

(SOUND: leaves skitter across the sidewalk)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

The blue weekly pill container rests open on the table. MA watches television. Nick stands over NARRATOR, fists flexing.

(SOUND: television game-show buzzer, wall clock ticks)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I’m sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? With who?

NARRATOR
I met a girl. We had a couple dates. That’s all.

NICK
Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

NICK slams out. MA flinches at the door.

(SOUND: apartment door slams, picture frame rattles)

NARRATOR V.O.
I was starting to see Nick wasn’t a Viking hero. He was just a person. And I was just a person too.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

SIONNA sets plates of peach pie on the table. Nick enters in his suede jacket and stares at her offered hand.

(SOUND: forks scrape plates, television murmurs)

SIONNA
I’m Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you.

NICK
Likewise.

NICK
So, I hear you’re a university lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
I’m studying English and philosophy. I hope to teach.

NICK
It’s tough out there. School doesn’t always pay off.

SIONNA
Education changes people. Diversity helps us learn. We are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

Nick’s face tightens. NARRATOR watches Sionna with pride.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

NARRATOR
You know I will.

NICK leans close to NARRATOR before leaving.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

EXT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Nick, NARRATOR, BOB, and SAMMY step through Joe’s crowded entrance. Nick’s size clears a path. LINDSEY, Nick’s blonde ex-girlfriend, stands by the pool tables with her new boyfriend.

(SOUND: AC/DC blares, pool balls crack, bar crowd hollers)

NICK
Who’s she with? Who’s that asshole?

NARRATOR
Why does it matter, Bro? You broke up with her.

NICK confronts LINDSEY and her boyfriend. The room quiets.

LINDSEY'S BOYFRIEND
You got a problem with me?

NICK
You’re in my face like a little bitch.

NARRATOR steps directly between Nick and the boyfriend.

NARRATOR
Nick, listen. You don’t even know this guy. Leave it, man.

NICK
Keep out of it.

NARRATOR
Stop it! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick’s fist catches NARRATOR across the face.

(SOUND: fist cracks against skin, music drops beneath a shocked hush)

JOE and BOUNCER HARRIS grab Nick. Nick head-butts Harris and tears free as Lindsey pulls her boyfriend toward the exit.

(SOUND: chairs scrape, BOUNCER HARRIS grunts, door bangs open)

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

NARRATOR runs through cold streets, one hand to his bruised face. He reaches Sionna’s old duplex and collapses into her arms on the porch.

(SOUND: NARRATOR gasps for breath, a distant siren rises and fades)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick.

SIONNA
No one who really cares about you does that.

NARRATOR
I love him, though. He’s my brother.

SIONNA touches his bruised cheek.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. You can’t let him intimidate you from your purpose.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The old coffeepot smokes on the stove. MA cries in her chair while NARRATOR, exhausted and thinner, turns off the burner.

(SOUND: scorched coffeepot sizzles in the sink, MA sniffles)

MA
I can’t remember anything anymore. I’m no good for anything.

NARRATOR kneels and holds her hands.

NARRATOR
You’re great, Ma. I love you.

MA
Nick doesn’t love me. He never comes to see me.

NARRATOR
I’ll check on him. I’ll make sure he’s all right.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

Nick’s Werner Street apartment is wrecked with empty containers, papers, clothes, and cold coffee cups. Nick, unshaven and red-eyed, opens the chained door a crack.

(SOUND: security chain rattles, microwave hums)

NICK
What do YOU want?

NARRATOR
Ma is worried about you. I think it’s time we talked this out.

Nick lets him enter.

NICK
You ruined my life. People say my little brother tried to kick my ass.

NARRATOR
I didn’t start that story. I tried to stop you from hurting somebody.

NICK
You’re not the brother I knew. Now you’re some hot-shit college kid.

NARRATOR
I’m working my ass off and finding out how stupid I am. That’s it.

Nick studies him, then points a finger.

NICK
I’ll let it slide. But don’t you ever interrupt me in public again.

NARRATOR
Will you see Ma?

NICK
Yeah. Tomorrow. Tell her I’ve been sick.

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

Nick’s flaming Trans Am drives through an intersection with MA in the passenger seat, dressed for church. Headlights slash across wet pavement.

(SOUND: rain lashes the windshield, tires hiss)

NARRATOR V.O.
Just when I thought things were getting easier, everything went to shit.

A car barrels into the Trans Am’s side.

(SOUND: violent metal impact, glass explodes)

The Trans Am spins into a utility pole. Steam pours from the crushed hood.

(SOUND: car horn blares continuously, sirens approach)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

NARRATOR enters the silent apartment. MA’s chair is empty. The answering machine’s red light blinks. He presses PLAY with a trembling finger.

(SOUND: answering machine beep, refrigerator hum)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff’s Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

NARRATOR dials, breathing shallowly.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Your mother passed away at the scene. Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo Hospital.

NARRATOR stares at MA’s afghan and collapses into her empty chair.

(SOUND: telephone receiver falls, NARRATOR sobs)

NARRATOR
Die, you fucking jackass. Die.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - ICU - DAY

Nick lies pale and battered under tubes, one leg immobilized, oxygen beneath his nose. NARRATOR stands at the doorway but cannot enter.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeps, ventilator hisses)

NARRATOR V.O.
They said Nick had been drinking. He took Ma to church drunk. Then he killed her.

NARRATOR turns away from Nick’s room.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Sionna’s warm living room glows with candlelight. SIONNA sits alone on the brown couch, wearing a loose shirt over healing bandages. The front door opens. Nick, limping with a cane and carrying a switchblade, enters.

(SOUND: front door thumps against the wall, candle flame flickers)

SIONNA
Nick? I never would have expected you here.

NICK
You’re the reason I’m probably going to jail.

SIONNA
What makes you think I’m responsible?

NICK looms over her, furious.

NICK
You confused his stupid brain with school and crazy ideas. Stay out of his life.

SIONNA
Nick, calm down. This has nothing to do with me.

Nick grabs her throat and forces her against the hall wall. He flicks open the switchblade.

(SOUND: switchblade snaps open, SIONNA chokes)

NICK
Break it off with him. Get me cash. Or this blade goes into your little neck.

The front door crashes open. NARRATOR rushes in and grabs Nick’s arm.

NARRATOR
Nick! What the fuck are you doing?

Nick jerks free. The blade plunges into Sionna’s upper chest. Blood darkens her shirt as she slides down the wall.

(SOUND: SIONNA cries out, blade clatters on wood)

NARRATOR catches her, presses both hands against the wound, and dials the phone.

NARRATOR
Stay with me. Please, Sionna. Stay with me.

(SOUND: NARRATOR’s ragged breathing, ambulance siren closes in)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Months later. Sionna, thinner and blank-eyed, sits in a rocking chair with a foam brace supporting her healed shoulder. NARRATOR sits across from her, desperate.

(SOUND: rocking chair creaks, rain taps the window)

NARRATOR
You know I love you, right?

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

NARRATOR kneels before her.

NARRATOR
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is doubt, I can’t go on with you. I need to go to Mom and Dad’s for a while.

NARRATOR’s face breaks.

NARRATOR
Please stay.

SIONNA walks slowly into the bedroom. NARRATOR remains alone.

(SOUND: bedroom door closes softly, rain grows heavier)

NARRATOR V.O.
She saved my life once. Then my brother took her away too.

EXT. SEATTLE WATERFRONT - DAY

Years later. NARRATOR, now older, broader, neatly dressed in a dark coat, walks through gray Seattle streets. He passes a bright sign: TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA.

(SOUND: ferry horn moans across Puget Sound, rain patters on awnings)

NARRATOR V.O.
I finished school. Tony helped me learn the business. Now I owned pizza places in Seattle.

NARRATOR pauses at a coffee shop window, sees a woman’s brown hair, and looks away.

NARRATOR V.O.
Seven years of change. Seven years of loneliness. Seven years of hatred for my brother.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles flicker around yoga mats. NARRATOR lies still in Savasana, eyes closed.

(SOUND: soft breathing, a distant rainstorm against skylights)

YOGA INSTRUCTOR ANIKA
Think of something you are grateful for.

NARRATOR V.O.
I had spent years blaming Nick, Sionna, myself. Holding resentment was its own prison.

NARRATOR opens his eyes, calm for the first time.

NARRATOR V.O.
I had to find forgiveness.

EXT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

NARRATOR crosses a gravel lot toward a severe brick prison. Cold wind pushes at his coat.

(SOUND: wind rattles chain-link fencing, distant gate buzzes)

NARRATOR V.O.
I had not seen Nick since sentencing. I did not know what I would find.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - VISITING ROOM - DAY

A plexiglass partition divides NARRATOR from Nick. Nick is older, scrawny, graying, and dressed in an orange prison uniform. He lifts the phone with shaking fingers.

(SOUND: fluorescent lights buzz, telephones murmur, doors clang)

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK
It’s not a cakewalk. I got my routine down.

NARRATOR takes a breath.

NARRATOR
I’m here to tell you I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing what you have to say.

Nick lowers his eyes.

NARRATOR
You killed my mother in a drunk driving accident. You held me down my whole life. You tried to kill the love of my life.

NICK
I know.

NARRATOR
But I forgive you.

Nick looks up, stunned.

NARRATOR
I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

(SOUND: a guard’s keys jangle, an overhead speaker crackles)

NICK cries without hiding it.

NICK
I’m so sorry. I’m a huge asshole. I think about Ma and Sionna every day.

NARRATOR
I want you to get better. I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
What happened with you?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’re expanding.

NICK
That’s fucking amazing. I’m proud of you, man.

A GUARD signals time.

(SOUND: guard knocks twice on the partition, metal door unlocks)

NICK
Bye.

NARRATOR
Bye, Nick.

EXT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

NARRATOR walks through the terminal with a small carry-on bag. He stops at the gate window, looking out at a plane beyond the glass.

(SOUND: boarding announcements echo, jet engines whine)

NARRATOR V.O.
The weight of thirty years was gone. I was free to live without fear, guilt, sadness, or judgment.

NARRATOR studies his boarding pass, then smiles faintly.

NARRATOR
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

(SOUND: boarding chime sounds, footsteps join the line)

FADE OUT.
